Chapter: TcoDirectArgReads

 A self tail-recursive loop whose third argument is `s.v + 1`, a field
 access plus a literal. The tail-call planner classes that argument as
 DIRECT, because is-direct-tail-binop accepts any left operand at all
 when the right is a small integer literal and the operator is add or
 sub. But tco-arg-reads walks only names, so it reports that argument
 as reading no parameters at all.

 It reads one. `s` is parameter two, and the second argument is a
 plain local, so the planner is free to write parameter two first --
 and then the field access reads the value that was just overwritten.

 The loop multiplies by ten each turn and adds one to the parameter it
 accumulates into, so the fault is a wrong number rather than a hang:
 correct is 101, and reading the clobbered parameter gives 1001. There
 is no diagnostic and no fault, which is what makes this shape worse
 than the tco-shuffle-spill one it sits beside.

Section: Fixture

  Box = record {
   v : Integer
  }

  mk : Integer -> Box
  mk (x) = Box { v = x }

  loop4 : Integer, Box, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  loop4 (i) (s) (acc) (n) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else let s2 = mk (s.v * 10)
   in loop4 (i + 1) s2 (s.v + 1) n

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("acc=" & show (loop4 0 (Box { v = 1 }) 0 3))
  end
